// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`index snapshots > normalized text of chart-v1.html 1`] = `"Weekly Active Users This chart tracks weekly active users across the first half of the year, pulled from the product analytics warehouse. Usage climbed steadily after the February onboarding redesign shipped. Figure 1: Weekly active users, Jan–Jun. Overall, the trend line supports continuing investment in onboarding rather than paid acquisition for the next two quarters, since organic activation is outpacing spend efficiency."`;

exports[`index snapshots > normalized text of chart-v2.html 1`] = `"Weekly Active Users This chart extends the weekly active user trend through the third quarter, pulled from the same analytics warehouse view used in the last update. Growth held through the summer months, with a brief dip in August around the planned maintenance window. Figure 1: Weekly active users, Jan–Sep. Overall, the trend line supports continuing investment in onboarding rather than paid acquisition for the next two quarters, since organic activation is outpacing spend efficiency. The August dip appears fully explained by the maintenance window and has already reversed in early September, so no change to the current roadmap is recommended."`;

exports[`index snapshots > normalized text of dashboard-v1.html 1`] = `"Quarterly Metrics Dashboard Snapshot of core business metrics across the last eight quarters. $4.2M Total Revenue 1,284 Active Accounts 2.1% Monthly Churn 96% Uptime SLA Enterprise revenue grew 14% year over year, driven by seat expansion. Growth was broad-based across regions, with the largest gains coming from renewed multi-year contracts. Quarter Revenue Churn Status Q1 2024 $0.42M Churn declined to 2.1% On track Q2 2024 $0.48M 2.3% At risk Q3 2024 $0.51M 2.0% On track Q4 2024 $0.55M 1.9% On track Q1 2025 $0.58M 2.4% At risk Q2 2025 $0.61M 2.1% On track Q3 2025 $0.66M 1.8% On track Q4 2025 $0.71M 1.7% At risk Generated --"`;

exports[`index snapshots > normalized text of dashboard-v2.html 1`] = `"Quarterly Metrics Dashboard Snapshot of core business metrics across the last ten quarters. 312 New Logos $4.2M Total Revenue 1,284 Active Accounts 2.1% Monthly Churn 96% Uptime SLA Growth remained steady across the last two quarters, with new logo acquisition accelerating in self-serve segments. Enterprise revenue grew 14% year over year, driven by seat expansion. Retention also improved slightly, helped by a smoother onboarding flow. Quarter Revenue Churn Status Q2 2026 $0.79M 1.6% On track Q1 2026 $0.75M 1.7% At risk Q1 2024 $0.42M Churn: 1.8% On track Q2 2024 $0.48M 2.3% At risk Q3 2024 $0.51M 2.0% On track Q4 2024 $0.55M 1.9% On track Q1 2025 $0.58M 2.4% At risk Q2 2025 $0.61M 2.1% On track Q3 2025 $0.66M 1.8% On track Q4 2025 $0.71M 1.7% At risk Generated --"`;

exports[`index snapshots > normalized text of react-style-v1.html 1`] = `"All estimates assume a two-week sprint cadence."`;

exports[`index snapshots > normalized text of react-style-v2.html 1`] = `"All estimates assume a two-week sprint cadence."`;

exports[`index snapshots > normalized text of report-v1.html 1`] = `"Platform Migration Report Prepared for the engineering leadership review, this report summarizes the outcome of the Q3 infrastructure migration and what it means for upcoming releases. Background The legacy system had accumulated years of technical debt, and on-call load had grown steadily uncomfortable. The migration was scoped to reduce incident volume & simplify deploys. Outcomes The team's velocity improved after the migration. Deploy frequency roughly doubled, and the median time to recover from an incident dropped from forty minutes to under nine. Every workstream was validated against its own acceptance criteria before rollout, and no workstream shipped without sign-off from at least two reviewers. "We haven't had a Sev-1 in six weeks — that hasn't happened since before the rewrite." — on-call lead Risks & Follow-ups A few items remain open. The billing service still depends on the old queue, and its own acceptance criteria have not yet been finalized with the finance team. Finish decommissioning the legacy queue consumers Backfill integration tests for the billing edge cases Document the new on-call runbook Next Steps The following sequence is proposed for the next two sprints: Freeze the legacy write path Migrate remaining billing consumers Retire the old cluster"`;

exports[`index snapshots > normalized text of report-v2.html 1`] = `"Platform Migration Report Prepared for the engineering leadership review, this report summarizes the outcome of the Q3 infrastructure migration and what it means for upcoming releases. Scope This revision narrows the scope to the services that were fully cut over during Q3, excluding the partial billing migration which is now tracked separately. Reviewers should treat the numbers below as final for the services covered here, and treat the billing figures as provisional until that workstream closes out. Background The legacy system had accumulated years of technical debt, and on-call load had grown steadily uncomfortable. The migration was scoped to reduce incident volume & simplify deploys. Outcomes Deploy frequency roughly doubled, and the median time to recover from an incident dropped from forty minutes to under nine. Every workstream was validated against its own acceptance criteria before rollout, and no workstream shipped without sign-off from at least two reviewers. Risks & Follow-ups The team's velocity improved after the migration. A few items remain open: the billing service still depends on the old queue, and its own acceptance criteria have not yet been finalized with the finance team. Finish decommissioning the legacy queue consumers Backfill integration tests for the billing edge cases Document the new on-call runbook Next Steps The following sequence is proposed for the next two sprints: Freeze the legacy write path Migrate remaining billing consumers Retire the old cluster"`;
